Good:
  mut .width(n:Nat):mut S->this.width'(WidthNat#n);
  mut .oval(WidthNat, HeightNat): mut Graphics;
  g.position(X#(+205), Y#(+85)).oval(Width#110, Height#110)
Bad:
  mut .oval(w: Nat, h: Nat): mut Graphics -> this.oval'(WidthNat#w, HeightNat#h);
  g.position(+205, +85).oval(110, 110)
Explanation: a method takes a plain number only when its name says what the number is (.width, .leftInset), so that we avoid .width(Width#3). When a method takes two or more numbers its name does not say which is which: the typed values carry the meaning, and swapping them is a type error instead of an unpredictable result.
